  "step_3": "The decision reason requires focusing on the least probable (i.e., lowest chance) outcomes of each lottery and comparing their favorability. In this case, that would mean identifying the 10% chance of 3000 in A and the 45% chance of 6000 in B as the relevant events. The individual does mention the 45% chance and the 6000 Euro outcome, but does not reference the 10% chance of winning in A, nor do they compare the two lotteries based on their least likely (but best) outcomes. Instead, the justification is based on the perceived large difference in payoffs and the subjective acceptability of 45% as a 'high' chance — more aligned with risk-seeking behavior or a 'maximax' heuristic than the specific logic of comparing least probable outcomes. While the outcome highlighted (6000 at 45%) coincides with the least probable favorable outcome in B, the reasoning process does not demonstrate awareness of the comparative structure required by the decision reason. The absence of reference to Lottery A's low-probability event and the lack of a rule-based comparison reduce the likelihood that the formal reason was used. Thus, while there is partial alignment in attention to a high-value, lower-probability outcome, the full logic of the reason is not reflected.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
